# Plumera site rules

## Language split

- **Python** — content builder, APIs (e.g. Corpus), sitemaps, generators, and other non-app tooling.
- **TypeScript / React** — interactive apps only (`npm run dev:app`). Do **not** deliver content pages via the SPA.
- Local Python env: `.venv/` (gitignored). App deps stay on npm.

## Content delivery

- Every content URL is a **full HTML document** (title, description, canonical, body in the file).
- Do **not** use a React SPA / `#root` client router for content pages (local or production).
- Do **not** set title, meta description, or canonical at runtime for content pages.
- **`dist/` is the deployable site.** Local preview serves that same folder as static files; do not add a separate preview rewrite layer.
- Build with the **Python** content builder (`npm run build:site` / `python -m tools.content_builder`).
- Source of truth for copy is Markdown under `content/`.

## Landings vs content pages

- **Landings** (`public/{locale}/index.html`) are hand-authored HTML with a distinct layout.
- The builder **copies** landings and other `public/` assets into `dist/`; it does **not** emit index pages from Markdown.
- `content/{locale}/core/index.md` may exist for editing reference text only; it is **not** a build input.
- All other pages (for now: updates, privacy, votw, …) **must** have Markdown under `content/` and are **emitted** as full HTML.

## SEO / localization

- **No hreflang** alternate link tags claiming translation pairs.
- Canonicals are **self-referencing only** (`https://plumerastudios.com/...` matching that page’s URL).
- Prefer **the same slug/filename** across locales for sanity (e.g. `privacy.md`, `cefr.md`, `votw/votw-prendre-a1/`).
- When body copy mentions a CEFR level code (A1, B2, …), link it to that locale’s CEFR page (`/{locale}/cefr/`). Do not put Markdown links in YAML frontmatter.

## Content layout

```text
content/
  {locale}/          # explanatory / UI language
    core/            # site pages — updates, privacy; index.md optional/reference only
    learn/           # learning materials for that audience
      votw/
```

- **locale** — language of the explanation / UI.
- **core** — structural equivalence across locales (same filenames); body copy is not assumed 1:1.
- **learn** — no required file parity across locales; `target` (language taught) is frontmatter, not a folder.
- Future locales include `ar`, `pt`, etc.

## Build / deploy

- Build: `npm run build:site` or `python -m tools.content_builder` (after `.venv` + `pip install -e .`) → `dist/`.
- Local: serve `dist/` with a plain static server (`npm run serve:site` = `http://localhost:4173`).
- Deploy: upload/sync `dist/` as-is.
- Sitemaps: root index pointing to per-locale sitemaps, generated from files that exist in `dist/`.

## Drafts

- Mark a page unfinished with `draft: true` in its frontmatter. It stays out of `dist/` and the sitemaps.
- Review one locally with `python -m tools.content_builder --drafts`, which emits drafts alongside everything else and logs each as `emit draft:`. The default build logs them as `skip draft:`.
- `--drafts` is the **same build**, not a preview mode: same code path, same output shape. Production never passes the flag, so publishing a page means removing `draft: true` — nothing else.
- Links generated from files on disk (e.g. the VOTW series index list) follow the same rule, so a draft build links drafts and a production build does not.
- Hand-authored landings are copied as-is and know nothing about drafts; a nav link there can point at a page production has not emitted.


## Do not

- **DO NOT MERGE PRs.** Never merge pull requests. No exceptions — not even if the user asks in that conversation. Prepare the PR, test it, report results; the user merges.
- **DO NOT CHANGE CONTENT.** Do not edit user-facing copy (Markdown under `content/`, landing HTML text, chrome/nav/footer strings, template labels, sidebar titles, etc.) unless that exact change is in the current plan or the user explicitly asked for it in this conversation. No drive-by renames, rewrites, or “helpful” wording fixes. No exceptions — not even if the change seems obviously correct or “helpful.”
- **DO NOT ADD LARGE FEATURES** outside the agreed plan or discussion. No exceptions — not even if the feature seems small, related, or an improvement. Code fixes and small scoped changes that implement what was asked are fine; new product surface, sidebar blocks, nav items, or other expansions that were not requested are not. When unsure, ask.
- Canonicalize one locale to another.
- Imply 1:1 correspondence between learn content across languages.
- Add SPA meta-patching for SEO on content pages.
- Emit landing `index.html` from Markdown.
- Rewrite content delivery in TypeScript when Python tooling should own it.
